---
// A short, quiet transition between the hero and the experience — a single
// editorial statement set in the warming Monet sky. Swiss restraint: one line,
// generous space, no ornament. Replaces the old (weak) painted-cloud section.
//
// THE FIELD IS NOW THE HEADLINE, on the owner: "for what I'm doing, maybe we should start right with what
// am I doing: Multi-period Portfolio Optimization. That's the tag line."
//
// It used to open with "a researcher by day, an artist by night" — a charming line that names three hats and
// no subject, so a reader arrived at the explainer without knowing what field it explains. Naming the work
// first is also what the reordering downstream is about: what I do, then how I got here.
//
// The identity line survives BENEATH it, smaller. It still carries the two doorways (/research and /art) and
// the site's stated hierarchy — quant first, engineer second — but it now qualifies a subject instead of
// standing in for one.
---
<section id="interlude" class="relative min-h-[78vh] flex flex-col items-center justify-center px-[8vw]">
  <!-- "An OR quant in multi-period portfolio optimization" rather than the bare field name, on the owner:
       "maybe it's smoother running An OR Quant in Multi-Period Portfolio Optimization." It reads better and
       it says more — the discipline he works IN (operations research) and the problem he works ON, in one
       breath, where the field name alone gave only the second. -->
  <p class="field text-center font-display text-ink-1 leading-[1.06] text-[clamp(27px,4.4vw,58px)]">
    An OR quant in multi-period<br />portfolio optimization
  </p>
  <p class="field-sub text-center font-mono uppercase text-ink-3 tracking-[0.2em] text-[clamp(10px,1.1vw,12.5px)]">
    deciding, and deciding again, under constraints
  </p>

  <p class="statement max-w-[26ch] text-center font-display italic text-ink-2 leading-[1.35] text-[clamp(17px,2.1vw,26px)]">
    <a class="research-link" href="/research">A researcher by day</a>, <a class="art-link" href="/art">an artist by night</a>, and a mathematician at heart.
  </p>

  <!-- The hierarchy, stated once. Quieter than the lines above: it qualifies them rather than competing. -->
  <p class="reasons text-center font-display italic text-ink-3 max-w-[34ch] leading-[1.45] text-[clamp(13px,1.4vw,17px)]">
    A quant who reasons in structure,<br />an engineer who makes the math run.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  /* Quiet doorway: ink text with an underline that draws in from the left on
     hover/focus. No color shift (the seal stays reserved for the seal mark). */
  .art-link {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    position: relative;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .art-link::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    bottom: -0.06em;
    width: 100%;
    height: 1px;
    background: currentColor;
    opacity: 0.7;
    transform: scaleX(0);
    transform-origin: left center;
    transition: transform 0.4s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1);
  }
  .art-link:hover::after,
  .art-link:focus-visible::after { transform: scaleX(1); }
  /* keyboard focus stays visible even where transitions are reduced */
  .art-link:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid currentColor; outline-offset: 4px; border-radius: 2px; }
  /* reduced motion / no animation-timeline: show the underline statically so
     the affordance is never lost, just without the draw-in sweep */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .art-link::after { transition: none; }
    .art-link:hover::after, .art-link:focus-visible::after { transform: scaleX(1); }
  }

  /* The "day" doorway → /research. A DIFFERENT affordance from the artist
     underline: a soft ochre highlighter wash sweeps in BEHIND the words on
     hover/focus (like a marker stroke), then the text rides on top. Transform-
     only; the seal red stays reserved. */
  .research-link {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    position: relative;
    white-space: nowrap;
    isolation: isolate; /* keep the wash's z-index local to this link */
  }
  .research-link::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: -0.12em;
    right: -0.12em;
    bottom: 0.02em;
    height: 0.62em;        /* a marker stripe over the lower part of the text */
    background: var(--ochre);
    opacity: 0.34;
    border-radius: 1px;
    z-index: -1;
    transform: scaleX(0);
    transform-origin: left center;
    transition: transform 0.42s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1);
  }
  .research-link:hover::before,
  .research-link:focus-visible::before { transform: scaleX(1); }
  .research-link:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid currentColor; outline-offset: 4px; border-radius: 2px; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .research-link::before { transition: none; }
    .research-link:hover::before, .research-link:focus-visible::before { transform: scaleX(1); }
  }

  /* ── A ONE-TIME HINT that the two phrases are doors.
     Both already have hover affordances — an ochre marker wash on "day", an underline draw on
     "night" — but neither ANNOUNCES itself, so a visitor who never hovers never learns they are
     links. This plays each affordance once, in sequence, shortly after the line scrolls in, and then
     leaves them alone: an invitation rather than an animation. Transform-only, and it never runs
     under reduced motion (where both affordances are already shown statically). */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .research-link::before {
      animation: hint-wash 3.2s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1) 1.1s 1 both;
    }
    .art-link::after {
      animation: hint-underline 3.2s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1) 2.0s 1 both;
    }
    /* Hover must win over the hint at any point, including mid-play. */
    .research-link:hover::before, .research-link:focus-visible::before,
    .art-link:hover::after, .art-link:focus-visible::after { animation: none; }

    @keyframes hint-wash {
      0%   { transform: scaleX(0); }
      22%  { transform: scaleX(1); }
      62%  { transform: scaleX(1); }
      100% { transform: scaleX(0); }
    }
    @keyframes hint-underline {
      0%   { transform: scaleX(0); }
      22%  { transform: scaleX(1); }
      62%  { transform: scaleX(1); }
      100% { transform: scaleX(0); }
    }
  }

  /* The quant line: same voice as the tagline, a step down in size and weight. Generous space above
     so it reads as a second beat rather than a subtitle glued to the line. */
  /* THE FIELD, as the headline. Display weight rather than italic: this is a subject being named, not an
     aside. Tight leading because it wraps to two lines at most widths and the two lines should read as one
     object. */
  .field {
    font-weight: 300;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    max-width: 22ch;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .field-sub { margin: clamp(14px, 2vh, 24px) 0 0; }
  /* Generous gap: the identity line is a separate thought, and running it close under the field would read as
     a subtitle of it. */
  .statement { margin: clamp(34px, 5.5vh, 68px) 0 0; }
  .reasons { margin: clamp(16px, 2.4vh, 28px) 0 0; }

  /* Four lines, arriving in reading order on staggered scroll ranges — the field first, because it is the
     subject. Same keyframes throughout, so there is only one animation to keep in sync. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .field {
      animation: statement-in linear both;
      animation-timeline: view();
      animation-range: entry 2% cover 30%;
    }
    .field-sub {
      animation: statement-in linear both;
      animation-timeline: view();
      animation-range: entry 8% cover 36%;
    }
    .statement {
      animation: statement-in linear both;
      animation-timeline: view();
      animation-range: entry 16% cover 44%;
    }
    .reasons {
      animation: statement-in linear both;
      animation-timeline: view();
      animation-range: entry 24% cover 52%;
    }
    @keyframes statement-in {
      from { opacity: 0.15; transform: translate3d(0, 28px, 0); }
      to   { opacity: 1;    transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0); }
    }
  }
</style>
